# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase — project "makemycake", region ap-south-1.
#
# Session pooler on port 5432, deliberately:
#   - the transaction pooler (6543) cannot run the DDL `prisma db push` needs;
#   - the direct host is IPv6-only.
# This one URL serves both migration and runtime, which is what prisma.config.ts
# and lib/db.ts both expect.
#
# Still Supabase Postgres — only the identity provider changed. `npm run role`
# writes UserProfile.role through this connection.
#
# Local only. .gitignore and .vercelignore both exclude .env*.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[project-ref]:[password]@aws-0-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=no-verify"

# ── Clerk ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Identity for /sign-in, /sign-up, /account, /kitchen and /admin. Both values
# come from the Clerk dashboard -> Configure -> API keys, and they are per
# instance: a development instance issues `pk_test_`/`sk_test_`, production
# issues `pk_live_`/`sk_live_`.
#
# Unset, the staff areas fail closed and everything that does not need an
# account — the builder, the price, the docket, placing a guest order — works
# exactly as it always did.

# Publishable. Meant to be in the browser and safe there: it identifies the
# instance and nothing more. It cannot read a user or mint a session.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="pk_test_..."

# Secret. NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, and never in a client component.
#
# Required by the running application, not only by tooling: clerkMiddleware
# verifies sessions server-side and throws `Missing secretKey` on the first
# request without it. scripts/role.ts also reads it, to turn an email into a
# Clerk user id when granting a staff role.
#
# It can read, modify and impersonate every account in the instance, so it is a
# deployment secret in the same tier as DATABASE_URL. Note this is a step down
# in privilege separation from the Supabase setup it replaced, where the app
# needed only a publishable key and no secret existed anywhere.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY="sk_test_..."


# ── Guest order tracking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Signs the cookie that lets somebody who ordered without an account
# open /orders/MC-… for that order, and only that order.
#
# The reference itself is NOT the authorisation and must never become one — it
# is read aloud down a phone line and printed on a docket. What authorises a
# guest is possession of a cookie this server set on their browser when the
# order was written, and this is the key it is signed with. Unsigned, anybody
# could type a reference into their own cookie jar and read a stranger's name,
# phone number and delivery window. See lib/guestOrders.ts.
#
# Unset, the key falls back to CLERK_SECRET_KEY and then to DATABASE_URL —
# both server-side deployment secrets of the same tier, neither ever sent to a
# browser, and an HMAC reveals nothing about its key. So guest tracking works
# on day one rather than staying quietly off. Set this explicitly if you want
# to rotate it independently; rotating it signs out every outstanding tracking
# cookie and costs nothing else.
#
# With no secret of any kind on the deployment, orders are still taken and
# guest tracking is simply not offered — rather than offered unsigned.
# ORDER_TOKEN_SECRET="a-long-random-string"



# ── Optional, and what each one changes ────────────────────────────────────
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN
#   SECRET. SERVER ONLY. Optional. Vercel Blob, created by attaching a Blob
#   store to the project; Vercel injects it. Without it the admin's photo
#   uploader says so plainly and everything else on that page still works.
#
#   Two things upload through it: catalogue option photos (stored under
#   `catalog/<category>/`) and cake photographs from /admin/cakes (stored under
#   `catalog/cakes/`). Both prefixes are covered by the one remotePattern in
#   next.config.ts, so no image configuration changes when a cake gets a photo.
#
#   Deliberately Vercel Blob and not Supabase Storage, even though the database
#   is Supabase: Storage would need `supabase-js` back in the bundle and a
#   service-role key in the environment, which is a higher-privilege secret
#   than this application holds anywhere. See the note at the top of
#   lib/storage.ts.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL
#   PUBLIC. BUILD TIME. Optional. The canonical origin that relative URLs in
#   page metadata resolve against. Falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL,
#   which Vercel sets, so a normal deployment needs nothing here.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FSSAI_LICENCE
#   PUBLIC. BUILD TIME. Optional. Printed on the docket and in the shop footer.
#   A licence number is published on the packaging by law; it is not a secret.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_BUILDER_ENABLED
#   PUBLIC. BUILD TIME. Optional, and OFF in production on purpose. `true`
#   reopens the 3D cake builder, which is held back — see lib/flags.ts. Leave it
#   unset and /build/* answers "3D Cake Builder — Coming Soon".
#
# E2E_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_DB
#   NOT AN APPLICATION VARIABLE. Read only by playwright.config.ts, and never
#   set it on a deployment.
#
#   The end-to-end suite places real orders, and playwright.config.ts loads this
#   very file — which on a developer's machine holds the *production* connection
#   string, because Prisma and `npm run role` need it there. So the config
#   refuses to start a browser unless DATABASE_URL names a host on this machine.
#
#   Point DATABASE_URL at a scratch database instead:
#
#     docker run --rm -d -p 5433:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres postgres:17
#     export DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/postgres
#     npm run db:deploy && npm run db:seed && npm run e2e
#
#   If the target really is a throwaway Supabase project or a branch — which is
#   legitimate and is not local — say so on the command that is about to write:
#
#     E2E_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_DB=yes-this-is-a-scratch-database npm run e2e


# ── Removed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Delete all six from .env and from the deployment. Nothing reads them.
#
#   ADMIN_USER  ADMIN_PASSWORD  KITCHEN_USER  KITCHEN_PASSWORD
#       The HTTP Basic credentials the two staff areas used to sit behind: one
#       shared password each, no way to tell one baker from another, no way to
#       revoke one person.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL  NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#       Supabase Auth, which Clerk replaced. DATABASE_URL above is unaffected —
#       the database is still Supabase Postgres, and only the identity provider
#       changed.
#
# docs/AUTH.md has the whole of it.
